    "symmetry_breakdown": {
      "flushes": "Identical across QJ, QT, Q9 (all 2 red cards vs 2 black aces).",
      "pairs_and_two_pairs": "Single pairs (Jacks, Tens, Nines) never beat Aces. Two pair, trips, and full house combinations are completely symmetric between J and T against AA because no rank exists between J and T, and AA holds the dominant overpair across all non-trips boards.",
      "straights_the_decisive_factor": "In Texas Hold'em, straights can be completed using one hole card. A Jack can only be part of 4 straights (A-K-Q-J-T down to J-T-9-8-7), and EVERY straight containing a Jack also contains a Ten. A Ten, however, can be part of 5 straights (A-K-Q-J-T down to T-9-8-7-6). On runouts containing 9-8-7-6-x, QT makes a Ten-high straight to beat AA. QJ cannot make any straight on this board and loses. Because straights cap at the Ace, QJ has no equivalent straight at the top end.",
