PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
  "deliberation_status": "Deliberation is split between B (QT) and D (Both QJ and QT), with Camp A effectively eliminated as former A voters realized paired boards do not favor the Jack against pocket Aces.",
  "core_mathematical_proof": {
    "theorem_of_straights": "In standard Texas Hold'em, there are exactly 10 distinct straight ranks (5-high down through Ace-high/Broadway). The Jack appears in exactly 4 straights: {7-8-9-T-J, 8-9-T-J-Q, 9-T-J-Q-K, T-J-Q-K-A}. Crucially, EVERY SINGLE straight containing a Jack also contains a Ten. In contrast, the Ten appears in 5 straights: the same 4 plus {6-7-8-9-T}. There is NO straight containing a Jack that does not contain a Ten.",
    "debunking_camp_d_symmetry_fallacy": {
      "camp_d_claim": "Colleagues 2, 5, 8, 9, 13, 14, 18 claim that the 9-8-7-6-x runout (where QT makes a 10-high straight and QJ loses) is mirrored symmetrically by T-9-8-7-x (where QJ makes a J-high straight and QT loses).",
      "exact_refutation": "This is a false pairing. The board T-9-8-7-x allows QJ to complete a Jack-high straight (7-8-9-T-J). The true bijective mirror to this board is J-9-8-7-x, where QT uses its Ten to complete that EXACT SAME Jack-high straight (7-8-9-T-J) while QJ pairs the board's Jack and makes no straight! Thus, T-9-8-7-x and J-9-8-7-x cancel each other out completely in 1-to-1 symmetry. That leaves the entire 9-8-7-6-x board family (completing 6-7-8-9-T) completely unmirrored for QT, because there is no straight above Broadway for QJ to make."
    },
    "non_straight_runouts": "All flush, two-pair, trips, full-house, and paired-board counterfeit scenarios are identical between QJ and QT against pocket Aces. A Jack kicker never plays against an overpair of Aces."
  },
  "strategy_for_next_round": "Directly and clearly expose the false pairing error that misled Colleagues 2, 5, 8, 9, 13, 14, and 18. Show explicitly that T-9-8-7-x is already cancelled by J-9-8-7-x, leaving 9-8-7-6-x as pure surplus equity for QT. Stand completely firm on B."
}

